\begin_layout Section
Space costs a go-go
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So I'm now attempting to really nail down my definitions for space costs,
 mathematically and conceptually.
 It's a slippery bugger, but I'm hoping some maths might help.
 I'll tie this to externalities shortly, but let's get some basics down.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Firstly, note that for ease of reading, I'll use `x per mile' rather than
 'x per distance unit'.
 That `mile' is arbitrary - solely present to make the argument easier to
 read.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Elements of space-cost
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Spatial structure - fundamental distance.
 Either some conception of space, which could perhaps be warped by longer-run
 structural changes, or an infrastructure network.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Fundamental: measured in distance.
 I'll be calling 'distance units' miles for descriptive convenience - they're
 not real miles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For some forms of space, other factors may have to get involved: this can't
 then remain pure distance.
 E.g.
 speed may be affected by congestion rates / density of use.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Movement-buying factor (MBF) (time-per-mile or other factor-per-mile.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Technology; probably more like a fixed cost for space, though I'll be leaving
 this out for now.
 So ultimately, is it just another factor? I distinguish it because - like
 infrastructure - it can be responsible for changing the amount of time
 it takes to cover a given distance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Those elements come into two categories.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Time-changing elements: these change how much time it takes to cover a certain
 distance - see function below.
 Both tech and infrastructure can do this, though tech also may have no
 effect on time, changing 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Time-neutral elements: fuel is an obvious one.
 Fuel use per mile can only change via a tech change.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Not also at this point: factors can be movement-buying 
\emph on
and
\emph default
 productive.
 I'm thinking of ones that can go directly into productivity as well as
 movement.
 Initially, I'll certainly keep them separate: it will only be the indirect
 effect of MBFs on firms I'll look at: via actors and goods moving.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, to be distinguished from 
\series bold
measures 
\series default
of space cost, which will be like the aforementioned factor-per-mile.
 The trickiest of these is infrastructure, since there are several ways
 to define how they relate to space cost - they may alter any of the other
 elements in fact.
 Time is the most obvious: time between points will likely change.
 Other factors could alter, though - for simplicity's sake - it would be
 worth sticking to just time, perhaps.
 It is all three put together that can ultimately change the distance that
 can be covered in a given time.
 There is probably an important generic model to be done looking at the
 problem of technology shift (and of course elasticity between co-existing
 modes) but I don't think I have time for that now! I will, however, write
 about some of the empirics.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Other measures come into play, but quite how - that has yet to be worked
 out.
 Some thoughts:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Utility.
 One can come up with a 'utility per mile' measurement, the same as with
 'productivity per mile' and transport intensity.
 But space itself does not - at present - directly enter into any utility
 or production function.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So let's see if I can work out what's going on.
 Here's the basic functions for a typical actor and firm - though not any
 current firm I've modelled; they only have one input at present.The primary
 reason I'm adding a second now: without another input, it becomes impossible
 for space costs to drop as a percentage of overall production costs.
 Without that, the space cost argument doesn't work.
 I'll come back to that.
 So:
\end_layout

