\begin_layout Subsection
Getting space costs into utility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I need to work through some of the simplest examples to find out how space-costs
 can plug into utility and production, and how I should describe that.
 So - let's start with utility.
 We've said that 
\begin_inset Formula $u=f(x,y)$
\end_inset

.
 The 'budget' a person has to work with is, at base, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

 - but 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 needs converting into goods.
 Matters are complicated, though, because 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 can be converted into either goods or, in one model at least, money and
 then goods.
 (It may be convertable into goods, but at different productivity levels
 from economies of scale.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If I'm sticking with backyard definitions, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is literally the budget: 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 can be maximised with a budget of 
\begin_inset Formula $x+y=t=1$
\end_inset

.
 Space doesn't come into it because they're staying put.
 But what about working?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Well, let's stick with what's happening in twogoods1 for now, and we can
 come back to converting directly to goods later.
 In twogoods1, production and wage-earning are separate: from the point
 of view of the actor, they know nothing about the firm's productivity.
 They only know the wage.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Right, let's get some space into this shizzle.
 I can't quite think up a clean way to describe the distance between firm
 1 and actor 1.
 Can't just use a vector as distance might end up being defined in various
 ways.
 So.
 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{f1\&a1}$
\end_inset

, which says, `the distance between f1 and a1'.
 The 'miles' between the two, whether Euclidean or some network distance.
 The raw time will also need defining, as it's the upper limit on whether
 a firm enters into the considerations of an actor (at the moment - move
 options may change that.) So 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{f1\&a1}$
\end_inset

, which says `time to get between f1 and a1'.
 Another label, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{a1}$
\end_inset

can be commute time for 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 This is just two times the distance to the firm they are commuting to.
 Er - well, this would actually have to be the final one chosen.
 All very confusing.
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 by itself can be the chosen firm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
c_{a1}=2t_{a1\&f}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I'm only allowing actors to choose firms half a day away, since they need
 to commute there and back.
 So, we now also need to define the set of potential employers for an actor.
 
\begin_inset Formula $PE_{a1}=\{f_{n}...\}$
\end_inset

 (How to describe that it can have any, but not necessarily all, firms?)
 The set of firms is of course 
\begin_inset Formula $F=\{f_{1},f_{2},...,f_{n}\}$
\end_inset

 So -
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
for\, each\, f_{n}\in F\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
if(2t_{a1\&fn}<1)\, then\, f_{n}\in PE_{a1}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Which just says, unless the time it takes for actor 1 to get to a firm and
 back again (the commute distance) is more than 1, add it to my set of potential
 employers.
 All this does, really, is guarantee that there's some time left for working,
 and the actor can then use it to see if working is worthwhile.
 So, let's presume that actor 1 ends up choosing to work with firm 1.
 The amount of labour they have available for contributing is just:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
l_{a1}=1-c_{a1}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Which is their one day, minus their commute time.
 In twogoods1, they - currently - actually test splitting that remaining
 time between backyard and work.
 I suspect if I worked it out, there would probably be no benefit in doing
 that test, but I'll have to check.
 For now - since I'm trying to home in on space costs - let's stick to presuming
 that if they are working, they are 
\emph on
only
\emph default
 working.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's define a wage as `the amount paid for 1 day's input' - and for firm
 1 that is: 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{f1}$
\end_inset

.
 The amount of time an actor puts into a firm is their labour, so we can
 call that: 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{a1},0<l<1$
\end_inset

.
 At the moment at least, actors don't work for more than one firm so there's
 no need to specify that.
 So actor 1's actual wage is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
w_{a1}=w_{f1}*l_{a1}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

